#
# The control for `incomparable_condition_gate.guard`: the same operator over the same left-hand value,
# against a right-hand value it can be compared with.
#
# `Port > 999` against `Port: 80` is a comparison the evaluator makes and finds false. The gate closes
# and the body is not evaluated, exactly as in the incomparable spelling, and the exit code and the
# report are the same. Nothing is wrong with this rule, so the run must stay silent.
#
# That is what keeps the note off ordinary rulesets. Most gates in most rulesets close because they were
# written to, and a note that fires on all of them is one nobody reads. Pairing it with the
# incomparable spelling is also what shows the note is keyed on a comparison that could not be made
# rather than on the gate closing, which is the mistake worth guarding against: the two runs differ in
# nothing else.
#
rule gate_is_decidably_false when Port > 999 {
    Name == "definitely_not_this"
}
